-- Seed country-level cannabis regulatory briefings: Europe
-- Countries: AL, AD, AT, BE, BY, BA, BG, HR, CY, CZ, DK, EE, FI, GR, HU, IS, IE, IT, XK, LV, LI, LT, LU, MT, MD, MC, ME, MK, NO, PL, PT, RO, RS, SK, SI, SE, CH, TR, UA, SM

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'albania','country','AL','Prohibited; Decriminalization Debate',
'Cannabis is prohibited in Albania under Law No. 7975 on Narcotic and Psychotropic Substances. Albania has been a significant illicit cannabis producer, particularly in the Lazarat region prior to 2014 law enforcement operations. Government-backed eradication campaigns have reduced large-scale production. There is no medical cannabis program. EU accession discussions may influence future drug policy reform.',
'No legal patient access pathway exists. Cannabis-based medicines are unavailable through official Albanian healthcare channels.',
'Albanian physicians cannot prescribe cannabis under the existing legal framework.',
'Albania''s illicit cannabis cultivation has been significantly reduced through enforcement operations, but informal production and export continue. No licensed commercial market exists. EU accession aspirations create some alignment pressure toward harm reduction approaches.',
'EU accession negotiations may encourage drug policy modernization. Medical cannabis reform is discussed in some policy circles but no legislation has been introduced. Change is possible over the medium term as EU alignment develops.',
'State Police and the National Anti-Drug Coordinator handle drug enforcement. Ministry of Health oversees pharmaceutical regulation.',
'Albania Law No. 7975; State Police drug enforcement data; EU accession monitoring reports','Current as of Q2 2026','Annual','Country-level briefing covering prohibition status and EU accession policy context',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='AL' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'andorra','country','AD','Prohibited',
'Cannabis is prohibited in Andorra under the Qualified Law on Criminal Provisions. As a small co-principality between France and Spain, Andorra''s drug policy is influenced by its larger neighbors, both of which maintain cannabis prohibition at the national level. There is no medical cannabis program.',
'No legal patient access pathway exists. Cannabis-based medicines are not available through official Andorran healthcare channels.',
'Andorran physicians cannot prescribe cannabis under the existing legal framework.',
'No licensed cannabis market exists. Andorra''s status as a tourist and duty-free shopping destination creates some cross-border informal trade dynamics.',
'No reform is anticipated. Andorra''s small size and co-principality governance structure limit independent drug policy development.',
'Cos de Policia d''Andorra handles drug enforcement. Ministry of Health oversees pharmaceutical regulation.',
'Andorra Qualified Law on Criminal Provisions; Ministry of Health pharmaceutical policy; regional monitoring','Current as of Q2 2026','Annual','Country-level briefing noting prohibition in micro-state context',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='AD' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'austria','country','AT','Medical Legal; Decriminalized',
'Austria legalized medical cannabis in 2008 under the Narcotic Substances Act (Suchtmittelgesetz/SMG). Cannabis for medical use is available through pharmacies with prescription. Austria decriminalized personal possession of small amounts in practice through prosecutorial guidelines. CBD products with THC below 0.3% are available as food supplements. Austria is an active participant in EU medical cannabis regulatory harmonization discussions.',
'Patients access medical cannabis through licensed pharmacies with a physician prescription. Domestic production by licensed operators and imports are both available. Products include standardized cannabis preparations (Bedrocan varieties) and magistral preparations. Cost is partly reimbursed by social insurance for certain conditions.',
'Austrian physicians (Ärzten) may prescribe medical cannabis for conditions including chronic pain, multiple sclerosis spasticity, and chemotherapy-induced nausea. A Suchtmittelrezept (special narcotic prescription) is required. Specialist referral is typically expected for complex conditions.',
'Austria has a functioning medical cannabis market with licensed import and limited domestic production. Several European cannabis operators supply the Austrian market. The magistral preparation pathway allows pharmacies to compound cannabis products from licensed starting material.',
'Austria''s medical program continues to mature. EU medical cannabis regulatory harmonization is expected to influence Austrian frameworks over 2025–2027. Adult-use legalization is debated but not imminent under current coalition dynamics.',
'AGES (Austrian Agency for Health and Food Safety) regulates medical cannabis. Bundesministerium für Gesundheit (BMG) sets health policy.',
'Austrian Suchtmittelgesetz (SMG); AGES regulatory guidance; BMG pharmaceutical policy; EU harmonization monitoring','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against AGES official guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering medical program, decriminalization, and EU harmonization context',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='AT' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'belarus','country','BY','Prohibited; Strict Enforcement',
'Cannabis is strictly prohibited in Belarus under the Law on Narcotic Drugs, Psychotropic Substances, Precursors and Analogues. Penalties are severe, including lengthy imprisonment. Belarus aligns its drug policy with Russian Federation norms and has not engaged with cannabis reform. There is no medical cannabis program. Belarus''s political context under President Lukashenko makes reform discussion effectively impossible.',
'No legal patient access exists. Cannabis-based medicines are unavailable through official Belarusian healthcare channels.',
'Belarusian physicians cannot prescribe cannabis under the existing legal framework.',
'No licensed cannabis market exists. Belarus enforces strict drug prohibition aligned with Russian drug enforcement norms.',
'No reform is anticipated. Belarus''s political environment under Lukashenko, combined with its alignment with Russian drug policy, makes cannabis liberalization effectively impossible in the near term.',
'Ministry of Internal Affairs handles drug enforcement. Ministry of Health oversees pharmaceutical regulation.',
'Belarus Law on Narcotic Drugs; Ministry of Internal Affairs enforcement data; EMCDDA European monitoring (limited)','Current as of Q2 2026','Annual','Country-level briefing noting strict prohibition aligned with Russian drug enforcement norms',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='BY' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'belgium','country','BE','Decriminalized (1 Plant); No Formal Medical Program',
'Belgium decriminalized personal possession of up to 3 grams and cultivation of one female plant through prosecutorial guidelines (circular). There is no formal national medical cannabis program — Belgium relies on the Schengen-area pharmaceutical framework, allowing physicians to prescribe cannabis preparations on a named-patient basis through import authorization. A national regulatory discussion on medical cannabis formalization has been ongoing. Belgium is a significant transit country for cannabis from the Netherlands.',
'Patients can access cannabis-based medicines including Sativex and Bedrocan varieties on a named-patient basis via pharmacies under physician prescription and import authorization. The process is administratively complex and costs are generally not reimbursed. Some magistral preparations are available through compounding pharmacies.',
'Belgian physicians (médecins/artsen) may prescribe cannabis preparations on a named-patient basis for conditions including multiple sclerosis spasticity, chronic pain, and nausea. No formally authorized domestic medical cannabis product exists, requiring reliance on import pathways.',
'Belgium does not have a licensed domestic medical cannabis production sector. The import authorization pathway allows global medical cannabis products to reach Belgian patients through established pharmaceutical channels. Several major European cannabis companies supply Belgian prescriptions.',
'Belgium is expected to formalize its medical cannabis framework, creating a structured licensing and reimbursement system. The coalition government has discussed harmonization with EU emerging norms. Adult-use reform beyond the current decriminalization is debated but not imminent.',
'FAMHP (Federal Agency for Medicines and Health Products / Federaal Agentschap voor Geneesmiddelen en Gezondheidsproducten) regulates cannabis medicines.',
'FAMHP guidance on cannabis-based medicines; Belgian prosecutorial circular on decriminalization; EMCDDA Belgium national report','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against FAMHP official guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering decriminalization, named-patient medical access, and formalization prospects',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='BE' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'bosnia-and-herzegovina','country','BA','Prohibited',
'Cannabis is prohibited in Bosnia and Herzegovina under the Law on Prevention and Suppression of Abuse of Narcotic Drugs. Bosnia''s complex governance structure (Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina, Republika Srpska, and Brčko District) creates some variation in enforcement priorities, but all entities maintain formal prohibition. There is no medical cannabis program at the state or entity level.',
'No legal patient access exists. Cannabis-based medicines are unavailable through official Bosnian healthcare channels.',
'Bosnian physicians cannot prescribe cannabis under any existing legal framework.',
'No licensed cannabis market exists. Bosnia''s geographic position on Balkan drug trafficking routes creates some enforcement complexity.',
'No reform is anticipated in the near term. Bosnia''s complex governance structure and EU accession aspirations may eventually drive policy modernization, but cannabis reform is not a current priority.',
'Drug enforcement is handled by entity police forces (Federation Police and MUP Republika Srpska). Ministry of Civil Affairs coordinates health policy across entities.',
'Bosnia-Herzegovina narcotic drugs law; entity police drug enforcement data; EU accession monitoring','Current as of Q2 2026','Annual','Country-level briefing noting prohibition in complex governance structure',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='BA' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'bulgaria','country','BG','Prohibited; Industrial Hemp Sector',
'Cannabis is prohibited in Bulgaria under the Law on Control of Narcotic Substances and Precursors. There is no medical cannabis program. However, Bulgaria has developed a significant licensed industrial hemp cultivation sector, producing fiber hemp and increasingly hemp seeds and CBD-rich plant material for export. Bulgaria is one of the EU''s largest hemp cultivators, benefiting from favorable climate and agricultural infrastructure.',
'No legal patient access to medical cannabis exists. Cannabis-based pharmaceuticals are not available through official Bulgarian healthcare channels.',
'Bulgarian physicians cannot prescribe cannabis under the existing legal framework.',
'Bulgaria''s industrial hemp sector is a notable commercial success, with significant acreage under cultivation for fiber, seed, and CBD extract production. Bulgarian hemp products are exported throughout the EU. No medical cannabis commercial market exists.',
'Medical cannabis reform is under discussion as EU harmonization progresses. Bulgaria''s established hemp agricultural base positions it to expand into medical cannabis production if regulatory reform occurs, potentially by 2027–2028.',
'Bulgarian Drug Agency (BDA) handles drug control policy. Ministry of Agriculture oversees licensed hemp cultivation. Ministry of Health sets pharmaceutical policy.',
'Bulgaria Law on Control of Narcotic Substances; BDA regulatory guidance; Ministry of Agriculture hemp licensing data; EMCDDA Bulgaria report','Current as of Q2 2026','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering prohibition with substantial licensed industrial hemp sector',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='BG' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'croatia','country','HR','Medical Legal; Decriminalized',
'Croatia introduced a medical cannabis program in 2015, one of the earliest in the former Yugoslav states. The program permits the prescription of standardized cannabis preparations for qualifying conditions. Personal possession of small amounts is an administrative offense rather than criminal, effectively decriminalizing personal use. Croatia''s Adriatic tourism sector has generated some public interest in cannabis policy modernization.',
'Patients access medical cannabis through licensed pharmacies with a physician prescription. Standardized dried cannabis (Bedrocan varieties) and cannabis oil are available. Products must meet Croatian Institute of Public Health standards. Social insurance reimbursement is available for specific conditions.',
'Croatian physicians may prescribe medical cannabis for conditions including multiple sclerosis, chronic pain, epilepsy, and cancer-related symptoms. Croatian Medical Chamber guidelines support evidence-based prescribing. Prescriptions require specialist endorsement for most indications.',
'Croatia has a small but functional medical cannabis market supplied primarily through imports. Domestic cultivation licenses have been issued to several operators, primarily for industrial hemp but with some medical cannabis production capacity. The market serves a growing patient base.',
'Croatia''s medical program is expected to continue developing. EU harmonization and the country''s growing tourism sector may create further policy momentum. Adult-use reform is discussed but not imminent under current governance.',
'HALMED (Agency for Medicinal Products and Medical Devices of Croatia) regulates medical cannabis products. Ministry of Health sets clinical guidelines.',
'HALMED regulatory guidance; Croatian Institute of Public Health standards; Ministry of Health clinical guidelines; EMCDDA Croatia report','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against HALMED official guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering medical program and decriminalization framework',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='HR' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'cyprus','country','CY','Medical Legal',
'Cyprus established a medical cannabis program in 2019 through the Medicinal Products (Human Use) Law and subsequent regulations. The program allows licensed cultivation, processing, and prescription of medical cannabis products. Cyprus has actively sought to position itself as a Mediterranean medical cannabis hub given its climate, EU membership, and existing pharmaceutical sector.',
'Patients access medical cannabis through licensed pharmacies with physician prescription. Both locally produced and imported products are available. The program covers conditions including multiple sclerosis, chronic pain, epilepsy, and cancer. Patient access has expanded since the program''s launch.',
'Cypriot physicians registered with the Medical Council may prescribe medical cannabis for qualifying conditions. Specialist recommendation is generally required for initial prescriptions. The Ministry of Health has issued clinical guidance for prescribers.',
'Cyprus has attracted international cannabis investment due to its EU membership, favorable climate, and business environment. Multiple licensed cultivation and production facilities have been established. Cyprus is developing export capabilities targeting EU member state markets.',
'Cyprus''s medical cannabis sector is growing. The country''s EU membership and favorable growing conditions position it well for further export market development. Regulatory refinements continue as the program matures.',
'Pharmaceutical Services (Ministry of Health) regulates medical cannabis licensing and product standards. Cyprus Police handles enforcement.',
'Cyprus Medicinal Products (Human Use) Law; Pharmaceutical Services regulatory guidance; Ministry of Health clinical guidelines; EMCDDA Cyprus report','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against Pharmaceutical Services guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering medical program and Mediterranean hub development',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='CY' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'czech-republic','country','CZ','Medical Legal; Decriminalized',
'The Czech Republic has one of the most progressive cannabis frameworks in Central Europe. Personal possession of up to 10 grams is decriminalized (administrative fine only). A medical cannabis program was established in 2013, though initially limited by import constraints. Regulatory amendments have expanded access to include domestically produced cannabis. Czech society has a long tradition of cannabis tolerance and public polling consistently shows majority support for adult-use legalization.',
'Patients access medical cannabis through licensed pharmacies with physician prescription. Both domestic and imported products are available. Patient registration is not required. Health insurance reimbursement is available for epilepsy (particularly childhood epilepsy) and some other conditions. Out-of-pocket costs apply for most patients.',
'Czech physicians may prescribe medical cannabis for a wide range of conditions including chronic pain, cancer, multiple sclerosis, epilepsy, and palliative care. General practitioners can prescribe; specialist referral is not mandated. The Czech Medical Chamber has issued guidance supporting evidence-based prescribing.',
'The Czech medical cannabis market has grown significantly since domestic production was licensed. Multiple Czech companies hold cultivation and processing licenses. The market is competitive with both domestic brands and imported products from the Netherlands, Canada, and other markets.',
'Czech Republic is actively discussing adult-use legalization. Governing coalition parties have expressed support for reform, and a regulatory proposal has been under development. Medical program expansion and potential adult-use legalization before 2027 are realistic scenarios.',
'SÚKL (State Institute for Drug Control / Státní ústav pro kontrolu léčiv) regulates medical cannabis products and licensing. Ministry of Health sets clinical guidelines.',
'SÚKL regulatory guidance and licensed product database; Ministry of Health clinical guidelines; decriminalization framework; adult-use reform legislative tracking','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against SÚKL official guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering medical program, decriminalization, and adult-use reform trajectory',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='CZ' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'denmark','country','DK','Medical Pilot Program; CBD Legal',
'Denmark launched a four-year medical cannabis pilot program in January 2018, which has subsequently been made permanent through legislative extension. The program permits licensed import and dispensing of standardized cannabis products. CBD products below 0.2% THC are available as food supplements or over-the-counter products. Denmark has also licensed limited domestic cannabis cultivation for the pilot program.',
'Patients access medical cannabis through licensed pharmacies with a general practitioner or specialist prescription. Products include cannabis oils and dried flower from licensed producers. Health insurance reimbursement is not currently available, making cost a barrier for many patients. Patient numbers have grown steadily since 2018.',
'Danish physicians (læger) may prescribe from the approved product list for conditions including multiple sclerosis, chronic pain, and chemotherapy-induced nausea. Prescribing is not limited to specialists. The Danish Health Authority has issued guidance on appropriate prescribing conditions.',
'Denmark''s pilot program has established an import-dependent medical cannabis market. Danish Medic Cannabis has been a key domestic license holder. The program has attracted interest from international cannabis companies seeking market access.',
'Denmark''s permanent medical program is expected to expand its approved product list. Industrial hemp legalization with higher THC limits is under discussion. Adult-use reform is debated but not imminent under current political dynamics.',
'Danish Medicines Agency (Lægemiddelstyrelsen) administers the medical cannabis pilot program and regulates cannabis products. Ministry of Health sets health policy.',
'Danish Medicines Agency pilot program reports; approved product list; Danish Health Authority prescribing guidance; EMCDDA Denmark report','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against Lægemiddelstyrelsen official guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering permanent medical pilot program and CBD market',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='DK' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'estonia','country','EE','Prohibited; No Formal Medical Program',
'Cannabis is prohibited in Estonia under the Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act. Estonia has one of the stricter drug enforcement postures among Baltic states, with low-threshold criminalization. There is no medical cannabis program. CBD products with very low THC exist in a complex legal gray area. Estonia''s digital-first governance approach has not extended to cannabis policy modernization.',
'No formal patient access exists. Some CBD products are available through commercial channels in uncertain legal status. No medical cannabis pathway through the healthcare system exists.',
'Estonian physicians cannot prescribe cannabis under the existing legal framework.',
'No licensed cannabis market exists. The CBD sector operates under uncertain regulatory conditions. EU harmonization may eventually clarify CBD product status.',
'Medical cannabis reform discussions occur but no legislative action is imminent. EU harmonization of CBD regulations may create some change pressure over 2025–2027.',
'Estonian Police and Border Guard Board handles drug enforcement. State Agency of Medicines (Ravimiamet) oversees pharmaceutical regulation.',
'Estonia Narcotic Drugs Act; Ravimiamet pharmaceutical registry; EMCDDA Estonia report','Current as of Q2 2026','Annual','Country-level briefing noting prohibition with complex CBD status',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='EE' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'finland','country','FI','Medical Legal (Limited); Prohibited for Recreational Use',
'Finland has a limited medical cannabis framework under the Finnish Medicines Act. Cannabis-based medicines including Sativex (approved 2012) and nabiximols are available through prescription. A broader medical cannabis framework has not been enacted, and patient access remains limited compared to many EU peers. CBD products in food supplement form with THC below 0.2% are available commercially.',
'Patients access approved cannabis-based medicines (primarily Sativex for multiple sclerosis spasticity) through licensed pharmacies with specialist prescription. Special authorization (erityislupa) from Fimea is required for non-approved cannabis preparations. Patient numbers remain relatively low due to administrative barriers.',
'Finnish physicians may prescribe approved cannabis-based medicines (Sativex) without special authorization. Non-approved cannabis preparations require special authorization from Fimea, which adds administrative burden.',
'Finland''s cannabis medicine market is small and primarily Sativex-focused. Broader medical cannabis market development is constrained by the absence of a formal licensed supply framework. International cannabis companies have expressed interest in Finland as EU market access potential grows.',
'Finland''s conservative social policies make rapid medical cannabis expansion challenging, but EU harmonization pressures may drive framework development. A formal medical cannabis program broader than the current approved medicines approach is possible over 2026–2028.',
'Finnish Medicines Agency (Fimea) regulates medicinal cannabis products. Ministry of Social Affairs and Health sets health policy.',
'Fimea pharmaceutical registry; Finnish Medicines Act; special authorization (erityislupa) data; EMCDDA Finland report','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against Fimea official guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering limited approved medicines framework and reform prospects',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='FI' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'greece','country','GR','Medical Legal; Export Focus',
'Greece legalized medical cannabis in 2017 (Law 4523/2018, regulations 2018) and has made medical cannabis cultivation a priority sector for agricultural economic development. Greece offers licensed cultivation and processing with specific focus on export to European markets, particularly Germany. Greece''s Mediterranean climate and existing agricultural infrastructure make it an attractive medical cannabis production hub.',
'Patients access medical cannabis through licensed pharmacies with physician prescription. Both imported and domestically produced products are available. Greek health insurance (EOPYY) provides partial reimbursement for specific indications including multiple sclerosis and epilepsy. Product availability has expanded as domestic production has come online.',
'Greek physicians may prescribe medical cannabis for a broad range of qualifying conditions. Specialist recommendation is expected for complex conditions. Greek Medical Association has issued guidance supporting evidence-based prescribing practices.',
'Greece has developed a competitive licensed medical cannabis cultivation sector with multiple licensed operators producing for both domestic use and export. Greek-produced cannabis flower and extract is exported primarily to Germany and other EU member states. Several international cannabis companies have established Greek operations.',
'Greece''s medical cannabis export sector is expected to continue growing, particularly as Germany''s domestic cultivation ramp-up creates transition demand. Greece is positioning as a major Southern European cannabis producer. Further regulatory streamlining to support exports is expected.',
'EOF (National Organization for Medicines / Εθνικός Οργανισμός Φαρμάκων) regulates medical cannabis products and licensing. Ministry of Rural Development and Food oversees cultivation licensing.',
'Greek Law 4523/2018; EOF regulatory guidance and licensed product database; Ministry of Rural Development cultivation license data; EMCDDA Greece report','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against EOF official guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering medical program and export-focused cultivation sector',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='GR' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'hungary','country','HU','Prohibited; Limited CBD Tolerance',
'Cannabis is prohibited in Hungary under the Criminal Code and Act L of 2005 on narcotic substances. Hungary has one of the most restrictive cannabis enforcement postures in the EU, with the governing Fidesz party explicitly opposing liberalization. There is no medical cannabis program. CBD products are in a legal gray area and face inconsistent enforcement. Hungary has not aligned with emerging EU medical cannabis norms.',
'No legal patient access to medical cannabis exists. Cannabis-based pharmaceuticals are not accessible through Hungarian healthcare channels.',
'Hungarian physicians cannot prescribe cannabis under the existing legal framework.',
'No licensed cannabis market exists. Hungary''s political environment actively discourages cannabis sector development. CBD products are commercially available but face regulatory uncertainty.',
'No reform is expected under current Fidesz governance. Hungary is likely to remain an EU outlier on cannabis policy for the foreseeable future. Medical reform would require a fundamental change in governing coalition.',
'National Police Headquarters handles drug enforcement. National Institute of Pharmacy and Nutrition (OGYÉI) oversees pharmaceutical regulation.',
'Hungary Criminal Code drug provisions; Act L/2005; OGYÉI pharmaceutical registry; EMCDDA Hungary report','Current as of Q2 2026','Annual','Country-level briefing noting strict prohibition and political resistance to reform',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='HU' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'iceland','country','IS','Medical Legal (Limited)',
'Iceland permits medical cannabis use under the Medicines Act and associated regulations. Medical cannabis products can be prescribed on a named-patient basis with authorization from the Icelandic Medicines Agency. Personal possession of small amounts is treated as a minor offense in practice. Iceland''s geographic isolation and small population (approximately 370,000) limit market scale.',
'Patients access medical cannabis through named-patient authorization from the Icelandic Medicines Agency and pharmacy dispensing with physician prescription. Products are primarily imported. Patient numbers are limited by small population and administrative process.',
'Icelandic physicians may apply for named-patient authorization to prescribe cannabis-based medicines. No domestic production exists, so all products are imported. Specialist recommendation is typically required.',
'Iceland''s medical cannabis market is very small given its population. No domestic cultivation or production is licensed. The market is served entirely by imports under named-patient authorization.',
'Iceland may develop a more structured medical cannabis framework as EU and European Economic Area norms evolve. Population size limits significant market development.',
'Icelandic Medicines Agency (Lyfjastofnun) administers medical cannabis authorizations. Directorate of Customs handles import controls.',
'Iceland Medicines Act; Lyfjastofnun named-patient authorization data; EMCDDA Iceland report','Current as of Q2 2026','Annual','Country-level briefing covering limited named-patient medical access',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='IS' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'ireland','country','IE','Medical Legal; Access Programme',
'Ireland introduced a Medical Cannabis Access Programme (MCAP) in June 2019, initially as a five-year pilot. The program was made permanent through the Health (Cannabis) Act 2023. The program covers three conditions: spasticity associated with multiple sclerosis, intractable nausea and vomiting associated with chemotherapy, and severe, refractory treatment-resistant epilepsy. Products must be imported and meet quality standards. Ireland''s program has been described as one of Europe''s most restrictive medical frameworks.',
'Patients access medical cannabis exclusively through the MCAP with a hospital consultant prescription and HSE pre-authorization. Products are dispensed from designated pharmacies. The three-condition restriction significantly limits patient eligibility. HSE funding for approved patients covers product costs.',
'Access is restricted to hospital consultants (neurologists, oncologists, and other relevant specialists) who must register with the HSE to prescribe under the MCAP. General practitioners cannot prescribe under the program. Administrative requirements are extensive.',
'Ireland''s medical cannabis market is small given the restrictive three-condition program. Product supply relies entirely on licensed importers. Several international cannabis companies supply Irish patients through the import authorization pathway.',
'Ireland''s government is expected to consider MCAP expansion beyond the three qualifying conditions, particularly following the permanent program establishment. A broader medical access framework is advocated by patient organizations. Adult-use legalization has been recommended by an Oireachtas joint committee but is not yet government policy.',
'Health Products Regulatory Authority (HPRA) regulates medical cannabis products and import authorizations. HSE administers the MCAP. Department of Health sets health policy.',
'Health (Cannabis) Act 2023; HPRA regulatory guidance; HSE MCAP administration data; Oireachtas joint committee report; EMCDDA Ireland report','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against HPRA official guidance','Quarterly','Country-level briefing covering MCAP establishment, access restrictions, and reform prospects',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='IE' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

INSERT INTO cc_jurisdiction_briefings (jurisdiction_slug,jurisdiction_type,country_iso2,program_status,public_summary,patient_access,physician_access,market_dynamics,regulatory_outlook,regulatory_body,data_source_summary,verification_summary,update_cadence,coverage_summary,last_reviewed_date,watch_regions,change_notes,review_state)
SELECT 'italy','country','IT','Medical Legal; Home Cultivation Court-Permitted',
'Italy has a complex cannabis legal landscape. Medical cannabis has been permitted since 2007 under DM 9 November 2015 regulations. A landmark 2019 Supreme Court ruling (Corte di Cassazione) effectively permitted small-scale home cultivation for personal use, though Parliament has not codified this into legislation. Cannabis light (hemp with low THC) was a major commercial sector before regulatory uncertainty reduced market clarity. Italy is a significant medical cannabis importer and has a military-run domestic production facility (Stabilimento Chimico Farmaceutico Militare).',
'Patients access medical cannabis through licensed pharmacies (farmacie) with physician prescription. Products include standardized preparations from the military facility (FM2 varieties) and imported products. Health insurance reimbursement varies by region — some regions cover costs, others require full patient payment. Patient numbers have grown significantly.',
'Italian physicians (medici) may prescribe medical cannabis for conditions including multiple sclerosis, chronic pain, epilepsy, and palliative care. General practitioners can prescribe with a standard prescription form. Regional health authorities may issue guidance on prioritized indications.',
'Italy has a significant and growing medical cannabis market. The Military Chemical Pharmaceutical Plant produces domestic supply (FM2), supplemented by substantial imports. Canada, Netherlands, Portugal, and other exporters supply Italian pharmacies. The cannabis light (CBD hemp) sector operates in a complex legal environment following court rulings.',
'Italy is expected to expand its medical cannabis framework and increase domestic production capacity. Adult-use legalization has been discussed extensively in Parliament without passage. Further regulatory clarity on home cultivation and cannabis light is anticipated. Italy''s large patient base makes it one of Europe''s most significant medical cannabis markets.',
'AIFA (Agenzia Italiana del Farmaco) regulates medical cannabis products. Stabilimento Chimico Farmaceutico Militare produces domestic supply. Ministry of Health sets clinical guidelines.',
'AIFA regulatory guidance; Italian DM November 2015; Supreme Court ruling 2019; EMCDDA Italy report; SCFM production data','Current as of Q2 2026; verified against AIFA official guidance','Quarterly','Full country-level briefing covering medical program, court-permitted home cultivation, and domestic military production',DATE '2026-06-21','[]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'reviewed'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cc_jurisdiction_briefings WHERE country_iso2='IT' AND jurisdiction_type='country');

